# Project Memory

Every Traffic One project keeps a small, versioned `.traffic-one/` folder for
agent-readable context that survives across sessions, tools, and subagents. It
is not a dumping ground; keep each file short, current, and source-backed.

## Read Protocol

At session start or before non-trivial work, read memory in this order when the
files exist:

1. `.traffic-one/.agentignore` — respect it before broad file reads or edits.
2. `.traffic-one/product.md` — user, job-to-be-done, success metric.
3. `.traffic-one/stack.md` — selected stack and version pins.
4. `.traffic-one/rules/coding.md` and `.traffic-one/rules/security.md` —
   project-specific rules that override generic preferences when stricter.
5. `.traffic-one/known-issues.md` — avoid re-fixing known bugs in loops.
6. `.traffic-one/schema.sql` — current DB schema snapshot for DB-backed apps.
7. `.traffic-one/agent-log.md` — recent session summaries only; scan the tail.
8. `.traffic-one/mcp.json` — MCP server inventory; env var names only.

Then read `.traffic-one/plan.md`, handoff digests, graphify reports, and raw
source as needed.

## Baseline Files

Create or reconcile these files for new projects and existing projects before
normal feature work:

- `.traffic-one/product.md`: one-page PRD with user, job-to-be-done, core
  workflow, non-goals, and success metric.
- `.traffic-one/decisions/`: canonical ADR directory. Append new ADRs; never
  rewrite prior decisions silently. If legacy `adr/` exists, migrate or mirror
  into `.traffic-one/decisions/` when safe.
- `.traffic-one/rules/coding.md`: short project-specific coding rules. Keep it
  under roughly 150 instructions; do not duplicate the linter.
- `.traffic-one/rules/security.md`: non-negotiables such as no service-role keys
  in client/mobile code, no `user_metadata` in RLS, secrets only via env vars,
  and no production data in fixtures or docs.
- `.traffic-one/rules/AGENTS.md`: generated agent entrypoint composed from
  coding and security rules. Root `AGENTS.md` should symlink to this file when
  symlinks are safe; otherwise generate root `AGENTS.md` from the same source.
- `.traffic-one/schema.sql`: DB schema snapshot refreshed after every migration.
  If no database exists, write `Not applicable` with the reason.
- `.traffic-one/deployments.jsonl`: append-only deploy log. One JSON object per
  deploy with ISO timestamp, git commit, environment, actor, trigger, result,
  deploy URL when available, and rollback id. Never include secrets.
- `.traffic-one/known-issues.md`: open bugs, gotchas, and intentional deferrals.
- `.traffic-one/stack.md`: selected stack, package manager, runtime versions,
  provider versions, and important version pins.
- `.traffic-one/.agentignore`: files agents should not read or edit unless the
  user explicitly asks, such as secrets, huge generated files, vendored code,
  build output, reports, and binary assets.
- `.traffic-one/agent-log.md`: append a short session summary after meaningful
  work: what changed, decisions made, verification run, and next follow-up.
- `.traffic-one/mcp.json`: MCP server config inventory. Store server names,
  commands, scopes, and required env var names; never store token values.
- `.traffic-one/skills/`: reusable local skills or commands such as
  `security-check` and `deploy-staging` when the project needs them.

## Write Rules

- Keep `.traffic-one/digests/`, `.traffic-one/reports/`, and `graphify-out/`
  local/ephemeral unless the user explicitly asks to preserve a report.
- Do not place secrets, service-role keys, database passwords, raw customer data,
  or production connection strings in project memory.
- Append to `agent-log.md` and `deployments.jsonl`; do not rewrite history except
  to redact an accidentally logged secret.
- Refresh `schema.sql` after migrations and mention the refresh in
  `agent-log.md`.
- Update `known-issues.md` when a bug remains unresolved or a workaround is
  intentionally accepted.
